'''ammochryse''' (am′ọ̄-krīs), ''n.'' [< L. ''ammochrysus'', < Gr. ἀμμόχρυσος, a precious stone resembling sand veined with gold, < ἄμμος, sand (see ''ammite''), + χρυσός, gold: see ''chrysolite'', etc.] A soft yellow stone, found in Germany, consisting of glossy yellow particles. {{smaller|When rubbed or ground it has been used to strew over fresh writing to prevent blotting.}}

'''ammocœte, ammocete''' (am′ọ̄-sēt), ''n.'' A fish of the genus ''Ammocœtes''.

'''Ammocœtes''' (am-ọ̄-sē′tēz), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. ἄμμος, sand (see ''ammite''), + κοίτη, a bed, < κεῖσθαι, lie.] A generic name of a myzont or lamprey-like fish. {{smaller|(''a'') The young or larval stage of the petromyzontids, or lampreys, characterized by the want of eyes and by a semicircular mouth. During the period of this stage the animal lives in the sand of river-beds.}}
{{smaller block|This simple lamprey larva . . . was generally described as a peculiar form of fish under the name of ''Ammocœtes''. By a further metamorphosis this blind and toothless ''Ammocœtes'' is transformed into the lamprey with eyes and teeth.
{{right|''Haeckel'', Evol. of Man (trans.), II. 104.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') A genus of which the ''Ammocœtes branchialis'' is the young, which is distinguished from ''Petromyzon'' by the differentiation of the discal and peripheral teeth and the crescentiform dentated lingual teeth of the adult.}}

'''ammocœtid''' (am-ọ̄-sē′tid), ''n.'' One of the ''Ammocœtidæ''; an ammocœte.

